L = {}
L["widows-son"] = """Israel had a wicked king named Ahab, and his wife Jezebel led the whole nation to worship a false god named Baal. Baal was supposed to be the god of storms and rain. So God sent a prophet named Elijah to say something bold to the king. As surely as the Lord lives, there will be no dew or rain in the next few years except at my word.

The rain stopped. Rivers dried up. Crops failed. For a while God hid Elijah by a stream, and ravens brought him bread and meat every morning and evening. Then the stream dried up too. God told Elijah to go to the town of Zarephath, which was in the homeland of Jezebel, not in Israel. There, God said, a widow will feed you.

Elijah found a woman at the town gate gathering sticks. He asked her for a drink of water and a piece of bread. She answered that she had only a handful of flour and a little oil left. She was gathering sticks to make one last meal for herself and her son. After that, they expected to starve.

Elijah told her not to be afraid. Make me a small loaf first, he said, then make something for yourself and your son. For this is what the Lord says. The jar of flour will not be used up and the jug of oil will not run dry until the day the Lord sends rain. She went and did as Elijah said. She gave away what looked like her last meal. And the flour and the oil did not run out. Day after day, there was enough.

Some time later, the woman's son became sick, and he grew worse until he stopped breathing. The woman was heartbroken. She said to Elijah, what do you have against me, man of God? Did you come to remind me of my sin and kill my son? Elijah didn't argue with her. He said, give me your son. He carried the boy up to the room where he was staying and laid him on his bed.

Then Elijah prayed out loud, and it was a raw prayer. Lord my God, have you brought tragedy even on this widow I am staying with, by causing her son to die? He stretched himself out over the boy three times and cried out, Lord my God, let this boy's life return to him.

The Lord heard Elijah's prayer. The boy's life returned, and he lived. Elijah picked him up, carried him downstairs, and gave him to his mother. See, he said, your son is alive. The woman said, now I know that you are a man of God and that the word of the Lord from your mouth is the truth.

Why does this matter? This is the first time in the Bible that someone is raised from the dead. And it happened for a foreign woman, a widow, someone with no power and no standing, in the very land where Baal was worshiped. Jesus later pointed to this story. He said that there were many widows in Israel in Elijah's time, but Elijah was sent to none of them, only to a widow in Zarephath. God's care reaches past borders and past the people who expect it.

What happened after? The drought lasted three and a half years. And in the third year, God told Elijah to go and face King Ahab, and that led to the showdown on Mount Carmel. The widow's son grew up. Her jar of flour was a small daily miracle that kept a family alive through the worst famine in the land.

What does this mean for us today? It shows that God sees people who think they are forgotten. The widow had one meal left and no one to help her. God sent a prophet to her door. It also shows what faith looks like when you have almost nothing. She gave first and trusted second. And it shows that you can pray honestly. Elijah didn't give God a polite speech. He asked a hard question and then asked for a miracle, and God listened.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God cares for the poor and the grieving, and that no one is too small for him to notice. And it points to Jesus, who met a widow at the town gate of Nain, touched her son's coffin, and gave him back to her alive."""

L["carmel"] = """Three years had passed without rain. The land was dry and people were hungry. King Ahab blamed Elijah for it, but Elijah said the trouble came from the king himself, because Ahab and his family had abandoned the Lord and followed Baal. Then Elijah told Ahab to gather all Israel on Mount Carmel, along with 450 prophets of Baal.

When everyone was assembled, Elijah stood in front of the crowd and asked a question. How long will you waver between two opinions? If the Lord is God, follow him. But if Baal is God, follow him. The people said nothing. Elijah had come to make them choose.

He laid out a simple test. Let the prophets of Baal build an altar and put a bull on it, but light no fire. He would do the same. Then each side would call on their god. The god who answers by fire is God. The people said, that is a good idea.

The prophets of Baal went first. They prepared their bull and called on Baal from morning until noon. O Baal, answer us! But there was no sound, and no one answered. They danced around the altar they had made. At noon, Elijah began to tease them. Shout louder! Surely he is a god! Perhaps he is deep in thought, or busy, or traveling. Maybe he is sleeping and needs to be awakened. They shouted louder, and in their desperation they hurt themselves, as their religion taught. Midday passed, then the afternoon. Still there was no response, no one answered, no one paid attention.

Then Elijah said to the people, come here to me. He rebuilt the altar of the Lord that had been torn down. He used twelve stones, one for each tribe of Israel. He dug a trench around it. He arranged the wood and the bull. Then he did something no one expected. He had people pour four large jars of water on the offering and the wood. Do it again, he said, and they did. A third time. The water ran down around the altar and filled the trench. There would be no doubt about where the fire came from.

At the time of the evening sacrifice, Elijah stepped forward and prayed a short prayer. Lord, God of Abraham, Isaac and Israel, let it be known today that you are God in Israel and that I am your servant. Answer me, Lord, answer me, so these people will know that you, Lord, are God, and that you are turning their hearts back again.

Then fire from the Lord fell. It burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones and the soil, and it licked up the water in the trench. When the people saw it, they fell face down and said, the Lord, he is God. The Lord, he is God.

Why does this matter? The whole story is about a choice, and Elijah wouldn't let anyone stay on the fence. The test was fair, and the answer was clear. Baal, the supposed god of lightning and storms, couldn't produce one spark. The Lord could do it with soaked wood.

What happened after? The prophets of Baal were removed from the land that day. Then Elijah told Ahab to go eat, because he heard the sound of a heavy rain. Elijah climbed to the top of Carmel and bowed down. He sent his servant to look toward the sea, and the servant came back saying there was nothing. Seven times Elijah sent him. On the seventh, the servant said he saw a cloud as small as a man's hand rising from the sea. Soon the sky was black with clouds and wind, and a heavy rain came. The drought was over.

What does this mean for us today? We might not bow to a statue of Baal, but we all face the same question. Who or what do we trust to give us what we need? Money, success, approval, comfort. Elijah's question is still fair. How long will you waver? It also shows that God doesn't need our help to prove himself. Elijah prayed one short prayer, and that was enough. And the small cloud shows how God often works. Elijah prayed and kept watching, and the rain started with something the size of a hand.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the Lord alone is God, and that he answers prayer. And it prepares us for what comes next, because right after this great victory, Elijah would run for his life."""

L["elijah-angel"] = """Elijah had just seen the greatest day of his life. Fire fell from heaven. The people turned back to God. The rain came. He even ran ahead of Ahab's chariot all the way to the city gates. He must have thought the worst was over.

But when Ahab told his wife Jezebel everything, she sent a message to Elijah. By this time tomorrow, I will make your life like that of one of those prophets. Elijah had faced 450 prophets of Baal without blinking. But this threat broke him. He was afraid, and he ran for his life. He went a day's journey into the desert, sat down under a broom bush, and prayed that he might die. I have had enough, Lord, he said. Take my life. Then he lay down and fell asleep.

Think about what Elijah was carrying. He was exhausted from the contest, the long run, and years of being hunted. He was also disappointed. He had expected the whole nation to change, and they hadn't. He felt like a failure and he felt completely alone.

God didn't scold him. An angel touched him and said, get up and eat. Elijah looked around and there, by his head, was a cake of bread baked over hot coals and a jar of water. He ate and drank and lay down again. The angel came a second time and touched him. Get up and eat, for the journey is too much for you. So Elijah ate and drank, and strengthened by that food, he traveled forty days and forty nights until he reached Horeb, the mountain of God. This is the same mountain where Moses had received the law.

There, he went into a cave and spent the night. The Lord asked him, what are you doing here, Elijah? And Elijah poured out his heart. I have been very zealous for the Lord. The Israelites have rejected your covenant, and I am the only one left, and now they are trying to kill me.

God told him to go and stand on the mountain, because the Lord was about to pass by. First a great and powerful wind tore the mountains apart, but the Lord was not in the wind. After the wind came an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake. After the earthquake came a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire. And after the fire came a gentle whisper. When Elijah heard it, he pulled his cloak over his face and went out and stood at the mouth of the cave.

God asked the same question again, and Elijah gave the same answer. Then God gave him something to do. Go back. Anoint new kings. Appoint Elisha as your successor. And then God told him something Elijah didn't know. I reserve seven thousand in Israel, all whose knees have not bowed down to Baal. He wasn't alone after all.

Why does this matter? It shows that even the greatest prophets get tired and afraid and discouraged. The Bible doesn't hide it. And it shows how God responds. He didn't send a lecture first. He sent sleep, food, water and a gentle voice. He met Elijah's body before he met his questions.

What happened after? Elijah went back and found a farmer named Elisha plowing a field with twelve pairs of oxen. He threw his cloak over him, and Elisha left everything to follow him. For the rest of Elijah's life, Elisha was his helper and friend. Elijah no longer had to carry the work alone.

What does this mean for us today? If you have ever hit a wall after a big win, you are not alone. Sometimes the best next step isn't a bigger effort. It's rest, a meal, and a friend. Sometimes God speaks in a whisper, not in a storm, and we have to be quiet to hear him. And when we feel like we are the only one, we may be wrong, because God often has more people than we can see.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is gentle with people who are worn out. And it points to Jesus, who invites everyone who is tired and carrying heavy burdens to come to him and find rest."""

L["chariot"] = """Elijah's work on earth was almost finished. God had told him that he would be taken up to heaven. Elijah and Elisha, his helper, were at a place called Gilgal. Elijah said to Elisha, stay here. The Lord has sent me to Bethel. But Elisha said, as surely as the Lord lives and as you live, I will not leave you. So they went down to Bethel together.

There, a group of prophets came out and asked Elisha, do you know that the Lord is going to take your master from you today? Elisha said, yes, I know, so be quiet. The same thing happened at Jericho. Elijah said, stay here, and Elisha said, I will not leave you. Fifty prophets stood at a distance and watched as the two of them walked to the Jordan River.

Elijah took his cloak, rolled it up, and struck the water with it. The water divided to the right and to the left, and the two of them crossed over on dry ground. It was the same river that Joshua had crossed with the whole nation, and the same kind of miracle.

When they had crossed, Elijah said to Elisha, tell me, what can I do for you before I am taken from you? Elisha could have asked for anything. He asked for something bold. Let me inherit a double portion of your spirit. In those days, the oldest son received a double share of his father's inheritance. Elisha was asking to be Elijah's true heir, the one who carries on the work. Elijah said, you have asked a difficult thing. Yet if you see me when I am taken from you, it will be yours. If not, it will not.

They kept walking and talking. Then suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire appeared and separated the two of them, and Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind. Elisha saw it and cried out, my father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel! And Elisha saw him no more. He took hold of his own clothes and tore them in two, which was how people showed deep grief.

Then he picked up the cloak that had fallen from Elijah. He went back to the bank of the Jordan, and he struck the water with the cloak. Where now is the Lord, the God of Elijah? he asked. When he struck the water, it divided to the right and to the left, and he crossed over. The prophets at Jericho saw it and said, the spirit of Elijah is resting on Elisha. They bowed to the ground in front of him.

Why does this matter? Only two people in the Bible are said to have gone to heaven without dying. One is Enoch, who walked with God. The other is Elijah. His departure showed that death doesn't have the last word over God's faithful servants. Centuries later, when Jesus was transfigured on a mountain, Moses and Elijah appeared and talked with him. Elijah was alive.

What happened after? Elisha went on to do twice as many recorded miracles as Elijah. He healed a general of leprosy, multiplied a widow's oil, and raised a boy from the dead. The work didn't end when Elijah left. It passed to the next person. And the last prophet of the Old Testament, Malachi, promised that God would send Elijah again before the great day of the Lord. When John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness, Jesus said that he was the Elijah who was to come.

What does this mean for us today? Elisha shows us how to follow someone. He refused to be sent away three times. He stayed close, and he asked for the best thing, not for riches but for the spirit to do God's work. It also shows that every generation hands the work to the next one. No one is meant to do it forever. And it reminds us that the people we depend on won't be here forever, but God will be.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God honors those who serve him faithfully, and that his work goes on from one generation to the next. It also points ahead to Jesus, who also went up into heaven and promised to send his Spirit on all who follow him."""

L["sennacherib"] = """Hezekiah was the king of Judah, and he was one of the best kings the nation ever had. He trusted the Lord and he tore down the idols. But in his time, the Assyrian empire was the most feared power in the world. Its king, Sennacherib, had already conquered the northern kingdom of Israel and many of the walled cities of Judah. Now he surrounded Jerusalem with a huge army.

Sennacherib sent his commander to stand outside the city walls and shout in the language of the people, so that everyone on the wall could hear. He mocked Hezekiah. Do not let Hezekiah persuade you to trust in the Lord. Has the god of any nation ever delivered his land from the king of Assyria? Where are the gods of the other cities we have taken? How then can the Lord deliver Jerusalem from my hand? The people on the wall were silent, because Hezekiah had told them not to answer.

Hezekiah tore his clothes and put on rough cloth and went into the temple of the Lord. He sent word to the prophet Isaiah, asking him to pray. Isaiah sent a message back. Do not be afraid of what you have heard. Sennacherib will hear a rumor and return to his own land.

Then Sennacherib sent a letter to Hezekiah that said the same thing, only in writing. Hezekiah took the letter, went up to the temple, and spread it out before the Lord. It is something you can picture. A king with a threat in his hands, laying it in front of God. Then he prayed. Lord, you alone are God over all the kingdoms of the earth. Give ear, Lord, and hear. Open your eyes, Lord, and see. Listen to the words Sennacherib has sent to ridicule the living God. Now, Lord our God, deliver us from his hand, so that all kingdoms on earth may know that you alone, Lord, are God.

Isaiah sent a message to Hezekiah that the Lord had heard his prayer. The Lord said that the king of Assyria would not enter the city, shoot an arrow into it, or build a siege ramp against it. He would go back the way he came. God said, I will defend this city and save it, for my sake and for the sake of David my servant.

That night, the angel of the Lord went out and put to death a huge number of soldiers in the Assyrian camp. When the people of Jerusalem got up the next morning, they looked, and there were dead bodies everywhere. Sennacherib broke camp and withdrew. He went back to Nineveh and stayed there. Years later, he was killed by his own sons while he was worshiping in the temple of his god.

Why does this matter? The Assyrians had beaten every army that stood in their way. Their own records brag about trapping Hezekiah in Jerusalem like a bird in a cage. But they never claim to have taken the city. The Bible tells us why. The Lord, who has power over every king and army, protected it. Sennacherib's mistake was thinking that the Lord was just another local god.

What happened after? Hezekiah got seriously ill around that time, and he prayed again, and God added fifteen years to his life. Jerusalem stood for more than a hundred years after this, until Babylon came. But the lesson of the night the Assyrians were turned back lasted for generations. God can rescue his people without a single sword drawn.

What does this mean for us today? We may not face an army, but we all get threatening letters of one kind or another. A bad medical report. A pile of bills. A message from someone who wants to hurt us. Hezekiah's response is a good pattern. He didn't pretend the letter wasn't real, and he didn't panic. He took it to God and spread it out and prayed honestly. He asked for help, and he asked for God's name to be honored in how it turned out.

That is why this story is so important. It says that no power on earth is greater than the Lord, and that he hears the prayers of people who are afraid. And it points to the God who says in the New Testament that nothing, no threat, no power, can separate us from his love."""
